---

He walked it down, as he always did. The academy's own bag went to Ostrand twice a day for nothing, and that was precisely why nothing of his ever went in it.

Out by the wing's side door, with his own key. The dark length of the north quadrangle. A nod to the night porter at the lodge, returned without the porter's eyes leaving his newspaper. Then the four long bends of the bluff road, down toward the lamps of the river.

At the foot of the last bend, by the toll board, two grey coats stamped under their lamp. He let the Path run on that stretch from long habit and felt their attention lift toward him and touch him and slide off like water off oilcloth. Neither of them would ever know that the one paper in a month worth carrying had gone by them in a satchel at arm's length.

Halfway along the river street it began to rain. It was a fine cold rain that did not so much fall as gather on him, on his shoulders and the brim of his hat and the backs of his hands. It was still gathering when he paid the courier's clerk by the fish steps from his own purse and watched the packet go into the canvas sack. It was still gathering an hour and ten minutes after he had left, on the last bend going up. He had made that walk five times this term and never once minded it, and he did not mind it now.

It was past the eleventh bell when the duty key turned in the wing's side door. There was a week's ledger of floor hours waiting, to be added up and set against the instructors' sheets before morning.

He added it up, four long columns, and got it right.

He got such sums right four times in five, and took care never to be right oftener, because a man who never slipped at figures was a man people eventually began to watch. Last week, in front of the desk clerk, he had carried a ten into the wrong column and been put right very kindly, and had thanked her, and coloured a little. Tonight there was nobody in front of whom to be wrong, so he was not wrong.

Then he pulled out the cabinet drawer where the term's carbons were kept. It held a carbon of everything that had gone down the coast since the term began: calendar extracts, floor issues, the list of the boy's associates, the first product with its line about a private notation habit, and each month after it. The drawer ran out on its runners without a whisper, because he had waxed them his first week.

The whole wing was silent. Far below the bluff, the river was going about its business.

He filed the report. Then he sat for a while with the file drawer open, not filing anything else.

---

Cael told Lira and Brom that night in Brom's room with the door shut. He sat on the end of the bed; Brom sat on the floor against the wall with his knees up; Lira stood at the window.

He told them all of it, plainly, in order: the grind that did not come, the read on time and the wrong shape, the Wind sent forward, Reydan's fragment on contact, the floor giving a third of it back, the landing beat in front of the jammed post. Then Jask, and the true short answer. Then the drawing, and the nine dots, and the ninth.

Nobody said anything for a while when he had finished.

"Your arm," said Lira at last, with her back still to the room.

"It hums."

"Hums."

"Like a bell after somebody's stopped ringing it. It hasn't stopped." He moved the fingers of his left hand to show her they moved. "It's not the ordinary ache. I don't know what it is yet. I've written it down."

Lira turned round then and looked at the arm, and then at him, for a long time, and he watched her decide not to say any of the first four things she wanted to say.

"Jask's walking about," she said instead.

"Jask's walking about."

"Good." She sat down on the window ledge with her bad leg out. "Then it was right. Whatever comes next."

Brom had his chin on his knees. "What comes next?"

"Something goes up the coast. It has to." Cael told them the rest of the page: another officer, perhaps; more coats at the road; a letter to Withrow. "It isn't him being wicked. It's what he was sent for. Lira, I don't think it's your terms. He hasn't touched my paper wrong. He'll write it down right."

"I know what my terms were," said Lira. "I said *wrong*. I'm not a child." Her eyes went past the glass to the dark where the ferry landing lay. "That's what makes it worse. Every time this man has a chance to be easy to hate, he does his work properly instead."

"So we wait," said Brom.

"We get ready," said Cael. "And then we wait."

Karis, when he found her in carrel eleven the next morning, did not wait for him to finish, and by noon she had a box: a plain pasteboard document box from the stationer's in Ostrand. Into it, in order, went every sheet the provision had ever produced about him: the enrollment record, Gault's baseline note, every drill sheet he had built and every countersignature on each, every floor issue, wrong and revised, every supervised hour since his first week on the bluff. Each was copied in her small hand where the original could not leave the wing, and each copy was dated and initialled and cross-referenced to the original's place in the wing's own files.

"If somebody asks," she said, tying the box's tape in a flat bow, "we hand them this, and it's already in order, and it's already boring. Nobody has ever been made to read a box like this and come out of it angry. They come out of it tired."

"And if nobody asks?"

"Then I've a very tidy box," said Karis, "and I'll sleep better for it, which is worth a morning."

The incident report itself came up through the wing three days later. By then the warden's account ran to four pages. The wing made two copies: faculty, and file. Cael, who read everything on the bluff with his name in it, asked at the counter for the copy he was entitled to, and read it standing at the end of the passage.

It named the pin, the collar, the setting, the nine years, the inspections passed. Its last paragraph asked for every retaining pin in every apparatus on the bluff to be drawn and replaced. The warden wrote the way she spoke, without one spare word, and he copied two of her sentences into the observation notebook to learn from.

He was in it once. Page two, in the warden's account of the afternoon:

*Enrollee (assay provision) present at bay edge; moved to intervene; received the release on a guard arm; no injury reported. Third-year not struck.*

He read it three times. Every word of it was true, and none of it weighed anything. *Moved to intervene.* Six feet in a blink, forward into the hazard, and the hall's own record had two words for it, and both of them were right.

Nobody asked him a single question. Not the warden, not the wing, not Gault. The Ash instructor added a note of three lines to say the enrollee had done well and quickly, and that was true, and it asked nothing. Of all of it, that was what he could not put down. He had spent the whole of his life being asked. On the one afternoon he had done a thing worth asking about, the bluff had written it down in eleven words and turned the page.

---

He watched the coats.

On the first evening after the pin, at the change of the fourth hour, he sat on the coping of the wall behind the second quadrangle with Lira. Together they watched the relief walk up the river street to the ferry landing: two grey coats going, two coming, on the bell, the same as the night before and every night since the bridge.

On the second day the change at the road's foot was four minutes late. He wrote that down and then crossed it out, because one of the relieving pair had stopped on the way to buy a hot pie from a barrow and could be seen eating it.

On the third day there were two and two, on the bell.

Karis came out to the wall that evening with her own log, which she had kept since the bridge in a narrow book with marbled boards: every change at the ferry and the road's foot that any of the four of them had seen, the hour, the faces where faces could be told apart, a tick for on the bell and a small cross for late. She laid it open on the coping between them and ran her finger down a month of columns. Four late changes in a month, all four by under ten minutes, all four with a reason somebody had seen: a pie, a cart in the road, a dog, and once, memorably, a man who had stopped to help an old woman with a basket of eels.

"This is what nothing looks like," said Karis. "I wanted you to see it written down before you start reading something into the next pie."

He studied the columns, which were steadier than his. She had been counting the coats for exactly as long as he had and had never once told him so.

On the morning of the fourth day he woke, and lay still before getting up, and took stock of himself as he did before anything that mattered, and found the hum in his forearm gone. It had not faded. It had simply stopped, sometime in the night, the way a sound stops that has been going on so long you have forgotten to hear it, and the arm under it was only an arm again, a little stiff, a little bruised along the outer bone where the sheet had struck. He wrote: *Compression: the hum, gone on the fourth morning. Arm sound.* Then he went out to watch the coats.

Two and two, on the bell.

"Nothing," said Lira on the coping beside him, that evening.

"Nothing yet."

"You said four days."

"I said a change would show in four days." He watched the relief nod to the two it was relieving, polite as shopkeepers. "I didn't say no change in four days meant nothing went up. A report goes up the coast by courier. Somebody has to read it. Somebody has to decide what it means. Somebody has to decide what to do about what it means." He shook his head. "Four days was the soonest it could show. Not the latest."

"So we don't know."

"We don't know." He wrote the date and *two and two, on the bell*, and closed the notebook. "I'll know more in a week. Or I'll know that I still don't know, which is also something."

Lira looked at him sideways.

"You're very calm about it," she said.

"I'm not calm. I've only got one instrument, and it's slow. Shouting at it won't make it quicker."

On the fifth day the minutes of the faculty's session went up on the board by the theater door, as they always did, and Cael read them because he read everything. Under *apparatus*, Rooke had moved that the floor warden's schedule for replacing every pin on the bluff be adopted in full and at once. The bursar had proposed phasing it over a year. Rooke's ground was minuted in his own words: *the next pin to fail will not wait for the bursar's year*. The motion was carried. Under it, in Bracken's own hand, was a note that the registrar's office would find the money from the reserve within a fortnight and would account for it at the next session.

Jask's story, meanwhile, had grown in the refectory queue exactly as Cael had known it would. By the sixth day the assay boy had crossed the whole hall. By the seventh he had caught the post as it fell, though it had not fallen, and somebody on the lecture stair was telling it with a third-year flung through the air. Brom reported each version at supper with enormous solemnity, like a man reading out the weather from a far country.

"Today you lifted the post over your head," he said on the seventh evening. "With one arm. The bad one."

"Did I put it down again?"

"Nobody knows. The story ends before that."

Jask himself found Cael the next morning at the bottom of the lecture stair, waiting with his hands in his pockets and the look of a man who has been rehearsing a sentence and has lost it on the way.

"I didn't say any of it," he said, before Cael could speak. "The post over your head. The flying. I want you to know. I told it once, the first night, the way it happened, and since then I've not said a word, and it keeps getting bigger anyway." He scowled at the stair. "Somebody in the Force hall asked me yesterday if it was true you'd caught me in the air. I said I was sitting down the whole time. She said I was being modest."

"You were sitting down the whole time."

"I know I was. I was there." Jask rubbed the back of his neck. "I don't want to be the story. I want to be a Force third-year who reads his dial too much. That's all I've ever wanted to be on this hill."

Cael looked at him, and thought of a girl at the fifth bell in front of a board, and of a woman with a brass stud beside her name, and of himself on the lecture stair all season, with ninety people a morning deciding to look. Being the story was a thing that happened to people. Nobody he had ever met had asked for it.

"It'll pass," he said. "Something else will happen. Somebody'll fall off a rope frame, or Brom will walk into another wall."

"He walked into a wall?"

"He'll tell you about it. At length." Cael hesitated, and then gave Jask the one thing he had that might be worth having. "And the dial. You read it after every declaration. If the number's low, you hit the next one harder, and you do that all session, and by the last third you're fighting a needle instead of a post."

Jask stared at him. "How do you know that?"

"I wrote it down. Weeks ago. I write everybody down." He shrugged with his good shoulder. "Turn the dial to the wall for a set. See what you do when you can't see it."

Jask considered this for a long moment with his mouth slightly open, like a man being handed back a coin he had not known he had dropped.

His mouth stayed open a moment. "Odd thing to know about somebody."

"I know," said Cael. "I'm the assay one."

Jask laughed, a short startled bark that echoed up the stair, and went off toward the Force hall shaking his head, and the following week, Cael heard, he turned his dial to the wall for every set and moved up two lines on his own term's ledger, and told nobody why.

---

Eight days after the pin came the fifteenth session, and for a week beforehand there was nobody on the bluff who did not know what it meant.

Lira sent her challenge up to the top line on the morning the cards were made, in her own hand, on the clerk's slip: her name, the arrow, and the name with the brass stud beside it. She did it before breakfast and told nobody, and by the time the three of them came into the refectory, half the room had already read the board.

On paper it was a challenge upward of a single place, the only kind there was, from the second line of the standings to the first. Underneath that it was the seeding of the final, since whoever held the top line at the season's close fought her semifinal on the earlier day and came to the final rested. And underneath that, it was a thing Cael found he could only say plainly in the observation notebook, on the night before, at the foot of three weeks of pages headed *Fiske*.

*Tomorrow is the first time all season either of them meets somebody the ladder didn't hand her. Everyone else Fiske has fought this year, the bracket built for her. Everyone Lira's fought, the bracket built for somebody else. Tomorrow neither of them is what the paper says, and both of them know it, and they're the only two people on the sheet who do.*

Lira found him in the north hall that last evening, after his hour, while he was rolling the cords. She had been running the sequence at half pace on the stone, the short fourth after the half-step, over and over, and she stopped and stood with her towel and asked him one thing.

"How long does she collect?"

"A minute and a half," said Cael. "The same as the first day. Near enough every time. Both her bad openings fall inside it."

"Every time?"

"Four bouts since you sent it. Four pages. A minute and a half, and then the doors start closing." He looked at her. "Why?"

Lira did not answer. She folded the towel in half and in half again, precisely, and hung it over her shoulder, and he saw her lips move once, as Karis's moved before a clause, on a number.

"Go to bed," she said. "You look worse than me."

He had watched Fiske, Force Path, Copper Rank Eight on a certificate two years old, fight four times since Lira's decision, as Lira had wanted him to. Four pages. On every one of them the same thing: a minute and a half of collecting, two bad openings, and then the floor closing behind her opponents like doors in a long corridor, one after another, until there was nowhere left to stand. Fiske never struck anybody. She put her force into the boards where they were about to be, and they arrived on it, and stumbled, and found her hand on them.

On the evening before the bout, crossing the covered walk, Lira and Fiske passed each other under the north stair, in front of the board where both their names stood one above the other.

Cael was ten paces behind and saw it; neither of them stopped walking.

"Fifteenth," said Fiske.

"Fifteenth," said Lira.

That was the whole of it. Fiske went on toward the halls with her hands at the small of her back, and Lira went on toward the residence with her weight on the ball of her right foot, and neither looked round.

The next afternoon the yard was full to the rail.

Cael had never seen it full, though he had seen it crowded, for Nyle and for the Shield fifth-year, with people standing at the backs of the tiers. This was something else. Every tier on all four sides was packed from the lowest step to the rail at the top, and people sat on the steps of the north stair and stood in the archways and leaned out of the windows of the lecture range opposite. The faculty box on the north side had every seat taken and three people standing at its back. The standings clerk, who sat at a small table by the long board for every session of every season, was standing up.

Withrow sat in the west gallery, upright and white-haired, with two people from Ostrand on either side of her whose coats Cael priced, without wanting to, at about a year of residence fees apiece.

He counted, because he could not help counting crowds, the Silver-sheet enrollees he could see on the tiers. Eleven. There had not been more than two at any Copper bout all season.

He did not look for the man with the forms. Twenty-five days now he had kept his eyes off the man. But the brass hood of the lamp hung where it always hung, at the edge of his eye. In its curve the upper east tier lay in afternoon shade, and in the shade, third in a knot of six, a small figure stood with something pale against its chest.

He looked away, at the floor, where the porter was chalking the ring for the last time, slowly, with the face of a man who knew that he was being watched by more people than had ever watched him do anything.

Lira was on the bench at the floor's edge with her towel round her neck and her eyes shut. Brom sat beside Cael on the east tier, very upright, as he sat in church. Karis was on Cael's other side with the grey notebook open on her knee at a fresh page, and she had written the date at the top of it and nothing else, and her pencil was not moving.

Fiske came down the north steps into the sunk floor without hurry, and went to her chalk, and looked at the door frame before she looked at the door.

The adjudicating instructor stood at the table and read the terms in a voice that carried to the rail. A challenge upon the holder's line. Four exchanges, each on the table's glass, each to the cleaner touch or called even. The bout to the practitioner holding more touches at the close of the fourth. A drawn bout to leave the holder where she stood.

The glass was turned, and the sand began to run.

"Begin," said the table.

---

## CHAPTER — editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-29.md

# Chapter 29 — Four Exchanges

Karis wrote one line for each exchange. Cael read them afterward on her grey page, four lines in her small upright hand, and found that between them they told the whole bout more truly than his six.

*One. L. spends nothing. Why?*

Before the word, Lira did what she had done before every bout since the Ironyard. She crouched at her chalk and laid the flat of one hand on the boards, as if asking them something, and stood, and rolled her neck once each way. The first winter, he had asked why. She had said it was so her hand would know the floor before her feet had to, and had refused to say another word on the subject for two years. Fiske, across the chalk, kept her hands clasped behind her and studied the boards between them, as a carpenter studies a frame before hanging the door in it.

At the table's word, Fiske gave ground.

She always did; Cael had four pages that said so. Her art lived in arriving early, at the place another fighter was about to be, and she could not arrive early anywhere until she knew the fighter, so for the first stretch of every bout she was only learning, and learning made her clumsy. She stepped back off her line in the first breaths, and then again, nearly over her own chalk. Two openings, exactly as the pages promised, and on the tiers a hundred people sat forward at once.

Lira let both of them go by.

She did not even look at them. She stood on her chalk for a long moment after the word, quite still, with her weight on the ball of her right foot. Then she began, at a walk, to run her evasion sequence.

Cael knew it the instant he saw the first step. Everybody who had ever trained beside her knew it. It was the drill she ran on the north hall's stone at half pace whenever her hip would let her do nothing else: eleven movements, always in the same order, a step left and a check, a turn, two steps right along an arc, a fold and a rise, round and back to where she began. She had run it ten thousand times. She ran it now on the yard's boards in front of eight hundred people at the pace of somebody showing a child how to tie a knot, with no Wind in it anywhere, and when she reached the end she began it again from the start.

Fiske watched it.

Cael watched Fiske watch it, and felt the back of his neck go cold. Lira was teaching her. Step by step, slowly enough for anyone to follow, she was laying out the order her feet liked to go in and the places they liked to go, and the woman across from her, who lived on knowing where people would be, was taking down every word. He saw Fiske's head tilt a hair at the turn. He saw her weight shift a hair toward the arc before Lira reached it, the second time round. The third time, Fiske's eyes were on the place where the fold would come before Lira had begun the step that led to it.

Lira tested her guard twice in passing, a hand going out low, a hand going out high, with nothing behind either. They were only part of the drill.

The sand ran down, and at the table every eye went to the glass.

"Even," said the instructor. "No touch. First exchange."

A groan went round the tiers like wind through standing corn, the noise of people who have paid for a fight and been shown a dance. Behind Cael somebody said distinctly, *she's thrown that away*. Down on the south tier the cohort sat in their dark knot and looked at one another and did not seem sure.

"Why?" said Karis, beside him, very low. The pencil sat idle where the line ended.

"She asked me last night how long Fiske collects." Cael kept his eyes on the boards. "I told her a minute and a half. So she gave her two minutes. She gave her everything she wanted to know, and every bit of it was true. That's her sequence. That's what she does when she's left alone."

Karis's gaze went from him to the page and stayed there, and the pencil did not move again.

*Two. F. closes the doors. F. one.*

---

Fiske took the second exchange, and took it the way a locksmith takes a door.

At the word, Lira went into the sequence again, at speed now, the first step left and the check. Fiske was already there. Not at Lira. At the place the step after the check would have gone. Her declaration went into the boards there, short and flat, without any show at all, and the boards there turned to something no foot could trust, a smooth hard slickness lying over the oak like oil on a stair. Lira saw it and broke off the turn.

Fiske had already shut the arc.


